INT. COMMUNITY BREAD COOPERATIVE - FORMER BUS DEPOT - MORNING

A long concrete bay where city buses once slept. Now: flour-dusted steel tables, a brick oven bolted into the old inspection pit, strings of laundry-line bunting. Tall windows turn the light the color of weak tea. The air smells of yeast and diesel ghosts.

ANSELMA DUARTE, 60s, forearms like rolled dough, sleeves shoved up, scores loaves with a razor blade. She works without hurry.

TOMASZ WIERZBA, 30s, soft-spoken, rimless glasses, weighs flour on a brass scale and writes numbers in a battered notebook.

HADIYA BELLO, 20s, apron over a nursing-school sweatshirt, shapes rolls and yawns between each one.

HADIYA
Do we have enough rye for Thursday?

TOMASZ
I counted four sacks.

ANSELMA
Three. Mrs. Pruitt took one for her church.

TOMASZ
She didn't ask.

ANSELMA
She never does.

HADIYA
Three is fine. Thursday's mostly sourdough.

TOMASZ
Thursday is the wedding.

ANSELMA
Thursday is the wedding and the school order.

HADIYA
Then we need more rye.

TOMASZ
Then we need to call the mill.

ANSELMA
The mill doesn't answer before ten.

HADIYA
It's nine-forty.

ANSELMA
Then we wait twenty minutes. Bread has taught me this.

Nobody argues. The oven ticks. Hadiya sets another roll on the tray.

The big bay door rattles. Light floods the floor.

WINSTON FAIRWEATHER-LEE, 70s, cardigan, cane, a tote bag of library books, steps through. He squints up at the rafters like a man visiting a former church.

WINSTON
I drove a Number 14 out of this depot for thirty-one years.

ANSELMA
We know, Winston. You tell us every Tuesday.

WINSTON
It's Wednesday.

TOMASZ
He's right, it's Wednesday.

HADIYA
Then why does it feel like Tuesday?

ANSELMA
Because Tuesday's bread was better.

WINSTON
(touching a table)
They've kept the pit.

TOMASZ
The pit's the oven now.

WINSTON
Mm. Good use of a pit.

He sets a library book on the table. Hadiya reads the spine, nods, hands him a warm roll. He eats it in two bites.

The oven ticks again. Anselma slides in another tray.
